        .element-header {
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            gap: 30px;
            margin-bottom: 30px;
            padding-bottom: 20px;
            border-bottom: 3px solid #9b59b6;
        }
        
        .element-symbol-box {
            background: linear-gradient(135deg, #9b59b6 0%, #8e44ad 100%);
            color: white;
            width: 150px;
            height: 150px;
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            border-radius: 10px;
            box-shadow: 0 4px 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.2);
        }
        
        .symbol-large {
            font-size: 4em;
            font-weight: bold;
        }
        
        .atomic-num-large {
            font-size: 1.2em;
            opacity: 0.9;
        }
        
        .element-title {
            flex: 1;
        }
        
        .element-title h1 {
            font-size: 3em;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
            color: #2c3e50;
        }
        
        .element-subtitle {
            font-size: 1.2em;
            color: #7f8c8d;
        }
        
        .info-grid {
            display: grid;
            grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));
            gap: 20px;
            margin-bottom: 30px;
        }
        
        .info-card {
            background: #f8f9fa;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            border-left: 4px solid #9b59b6;
        }
        
        .info-card h3 {
            color: #9b59b6;
            margin-bottom: 10px;
            font-size: 1.1em;
        }
        
        .info-card p {
            color: #2c3e50;
            font-size: 1.1em;
        }
        
        .section {
            margin-bottom: 30px;
        }
        
        .section h2 {
            color: #2c3e50;
            font-size: 2em;
            margin-bottom: 15px;
            padding-bottom: 10px;
            border-bottom: 2px solid #ecf0f1;
        }
        
        .section p {
            margin-bottom: 15px;
            font-size: 1.05em;
        }
        
        .properties-table {
            width: 100%;
            border-collapse: collapse;
            margin: 20px 0;
            background: white;
            box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
            border-radius: 8px;
            overflow: hidden;
        }
        
        .properties-table th {
            background: #9b59b6;
            color: white;
            padding: 15px;
            text-align: left;
            font-weight: 600;
        }
        
        .properties-table td {
            padding: 12px 15px;
            border-bottom: 1px solid #ecf0f1;
        }
        
        .properties-table tr:last-child td {
            border-bottom: none;
        }
        
        .properties-table tr:nth-child(even) {
            background: #f8f9fa;
        }
        
        .highlight-box {
            background: #e8f5e9;
            border-left: 4px solid #4caf50;
            padding: 20px;
            margin: 20px 0;
            border-radius: 4px;
        }
        
        .warning-box {
            background: #fff3e0;
            border-left: 4px solid #ff9800;
            padding: 20px;
            margin: 20px 0;
            border-radius: 4px;
        }
        
        ul {
            margin-left: 20px;
            margin-bottom: 15px;
        }
        
        li {
            margin-bottom: 8px;
            font-size: 1.05em;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="element-header">
        <div class="element-symbol-box">
            <div class="atomic-num-large">1</div>
            <div class="symbol-large">H</div>
        </div>
        <div class="element-title">
            <h1>Hydrogen</h1>
            <div class="element-subtitle">Nonmetal • Group 1 • Period 1</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="info-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
            <h3>Atomic Number</h3>
            <p>1</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
            <h3>Atomic Mass</h3>
            <p>1.008 u</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
            <h3>Electron Configuration</h3>
            <p>1s¹</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
            <h3>State at Room Temperature</h3>
            <p>Gas</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <p>Hydrogen is the lightest and most abundant element in the universe, making up about 75% of all normal matter. It is a colorless, odorless, tasteless, non-toxic, nonmetallic, highly combustible diatomic gas with the molecular formula H₂.</p>
        <p>Despite being placed in Group 1 of the periodic table with the alkali metals, hydrogen is a nonmetal and behaves quite differently from the other elements in its group. It was discovered by Henry Cavendish in 1766, though it was not recognized as a distinct element until later.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>Physical Properties</h2>
        <table class="properties-table">
            <tr>
                <th>Property</th>
                <th>Value</th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Melting Point</td>
                <td>-259.16°C (-434.49°F, 13.99 K)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Boiling Point</td>
                <td>-252.87°C (-423.17°F, 20.28 K)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Density (at STP)</td>
                <td>0.08988 g/L</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Density (liquid, at m.p.)</td>
                <td>0.0708 g/cm³</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Density (solid)</td>
                <td>0.0763 g/cm³</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Phase at STP</td>
                <td>Gas</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Critical Point</td>
                <td>-240.18°C, 1.293 MPa</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Heat of Fusion</td>
                <td>0.117 kJ/mol</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Heat of Vaporization</td>
                <td>0.904 kJ/mol</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>Chemical Properties</h2>
        <table class="properties-table">
            <tr>
                <th>Property</th>
                <th>Value</th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Oxidation States</td>
                <td>-1, +1 (most common)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Electronegativity</td>
                <td>2.20 (Pauling scale)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Ionization Energy (1st)</td>
                <td>1312 kJ/mol</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Covalent Radius</td>
                <td>31 pm</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Van der Waals Radius</td>
                <td>120 pm</td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>Isotopes</h2>
        <p>Hydrogen has three naturally occurring isotopes:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Protium (¹H)</strong> - The most common isotope, with no neutrons. It makes up 99.98% of naturally occurring hydrogen.</li>
            <li><strong>Deuterium (²H or D)</strong> - Contains one neutron. It accounts for about 0.02% of natural hydrogen and is stable. Used in nuclear fusion and as a tracer in chemistry.</li>
            <li><strong>Tritium (³H or T)</strong> - Contains two neutrons. It is radioactive with a half-life of 12.32 years and occurs in trace amounts naturally.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>Common Uses</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Industrial Processing</strong> - Used in petroleum refining, ammonia production (Haber process), and hydrogenation of fats and oils</li>
            <li><strong>Fuel</strong> - Rocket fuel, fuel cells for vehicles and power generation, potential future energy carrier</li>
            <li><strong>Chemical Manufacturing</strong> - Production of methanol, hydrochloric acid, and various organic compounds</li>
            <li><strong>Metallurgy</strong> - Reduction of metallic ores and heat treatment of metals</li>
            <li><strong>Electronics</strong> - Protective atmosphere for semiconductor manufacturing</li>
            <li><strong>Food Industry</strong> - Hydrogenation of vegetable oils to create margarine and shortening</li>
            <li><strong>Scientific Research</strong> - Deuterium used as a tracer and in nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy</li>
            <li><strong>Weather Balloons</strong> - Lighter-than-air lifting gas (though helium is preferred for safety)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>Biological Role</h2>
        <p>Hydrogen is essential to life as it is a major component of water (H₂O) and all organic compounds. In the human body:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Makes up approximately 10% of body mass (by mass) and 60% by number of atoms</li>
            <li>Critical for maintaining pH balance through hydrogen ions (H⁺)</li>
            <li>Essential component of proteins, nucleic acids (DNA/RNA), carbohydrates, and lipids</li>
            <li>Involved in cellular respiration and energy production (ATP synthesis)</li>
            <li>Plays a role in enzyme activity and metabolic processes</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>Production and Occurrence</h2>
        <p><strong>Natural Occurrence:</strong> Hydrogen is rarely found in its pure form on Earth due to its lightness and reactivity. It is most commonly found combined with oxygen in water (H₂O) and with carbon in organic compounds.</p>
        <p><strong>Industrial Production Methods:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Steam Reforming</strong> - The most common method, using natural gas (methane) and steam at high temperatures</li>
            <li><strong>Electrolysis of Water</strong> - Splitting water into hydrogen and oxygen using electricity (green hydrogen when using renewable energy)</li>
            <li><strong>Coal Gasification</strong> - Partial oxidation of coal to produce hydrogen</li>
            <li><strong>Biomass Gasification</strong> - Converting organic materials into hydrogen</li>
            <li><strong>Partial Oxidation</strong> - Reacting hydrocarbons with limited oxygen</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>Environmental Impact</h2>
        <p>Hydrogen is considered a clean fuel when produced from renewable sources (green hydrogen). When used in fuel cells, the only byproduct is water vapor, making it an attractive alternative to fossil fuels for reducing greenhouse gas emissions.</p>
        <p>However, most hydrogen today is produced from natural gas through steam reforming, which releases carbon dioxide. The environmental benefit depends heavily on the production method.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h2>Discovery and History</h2>
        <p>Hydrogen was first produced in the laboratory by Henry Cavendish in 1766 when he reacted metals with acids. However, he did not recognize it as a distinct element, referring to it as "inflammable air."</p>
        <p>In 1783, Antoine Lavoisier named the element hydrogen (from Greek hydro genes, meaning "water former") after demonstrating that water was composed of hydrogen and oxygen. The element was first liquefied by James Dewar in 1898.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="highlight-box">
        <h3>Interesting Facts</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Hydrogen is the most abundant element in the universe, making up about 75% of all matter by mass</li>
            <li>The sun fuses about 600 million tons of hydrogen into helium every second</li>
            <li>Hydrogen gas is the lightest gas, with a density of only 0.09 g/L at standard conditions</li>
            <li>The name "hydrogen" comes from Greek words meaning "water-former"</li>
            <li>Liquid hydrogen is used as rocket fuel, including in NASA's Space Launch System</li>
            <li>Hydrogen has the highest energy content per unit of weight of any fuel</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="warning-box">
        <h3>Safety Information</h3>
        <p><strong>Flammability:</strong> Hydrogen is extremely flammable and forms explosive mixtures with air at concentrations from 4% to 75% by volume. It burns with a nearly invisible flame.</p>
        <p><strong>Storage:</strong> Must be stored in well-ventilated areas away from sources of ignition. High-pressure cylinders require proper handling.</p>
        <p><strong>Asphyxiation Risk:</strong> While non-toxic, hydrogen can displace oxygen in enclosed spaces, creating an asphyxiation hazard.</p>
        <p><strong>Cryogenic Hazards:</strong> Liquid hydrogen is extremely cold (-253°C) and can cause severe frostbite on contact.</p>
    </div>
